  "content": {
    "id": "v2-54709-p15-practice-slot-6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "If $4-3n$ is positive and $2n+6$ is positive, what are the possible values of $n$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$-3<n<\\frac{4}{3}$",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4-3n&>0\\\\-3n&>-4\\\\n&<\\frac{4}{3}\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}2n+6&>0\\\\2n&>-6\\\\n&>-3\\end{align*}$$\nDividing by $-3$ reverses the inequality sign. Both conditions hold when $-3<n<\\frac{4}{3}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
